# Mercury Agent — deployment-image accepted-risk ledger (machine-enforced).
#
# This file is consumed by the BLOCKING Trivy gates in
# .github/workflows/ci.yml and .github/workflows/security.yml, which run
# with `ignore-unfixed: false`. Every entry below is therefore an explicit,
# enumerated, expiring acceptance — when an entry expires (exp:) the gate
# starts failing and forces a re-review; a NEW unfixed CRITICAL/HIGH
# finding that is not listed here blocks the merge instead of passing
# silently. This replaces the previous blanket `ignore-unfixed: true`
# posture, which waived the entire unfixed-OS-CVE class invisibly.
#
# Rules for this file:
#   * Entries require owner review; record component, reason, and source.
#   * Only no-upstream-fix OS CVEs belong here. Anything with a fixed
#     version available is remediated by the Dockerfile's runtime-stage
#     `apt-get upgrade -y` (the canonical fix path) and must never be
#     listed.
#   * Expiry is 90 days (the documented quarterly review cadence).
#
# Enumeration source: the blocking gates' own scan of the BUILT image
# (trivy 0.70.0 via aquasecurity/trivy-action@v0.36.0 — the version the
# enforcing workflows actually run). RE-ENUMERATED 2026-06-18 against the
# shipped Debian **trixie** base (`python:3.13-slim-trixie`, Debian 13.5)
# after the base-image migration off bookworm, by building the runtime
# image's OS layer and scanning it with that exact trivy version. The
# built image carries 8 distinct unfixed CRITICAL/HIGH OS CVEs (2 CRITICAL,
# 6 HIGH), each cross-checked against the Debian Security Tracker (trixie
# status "open", no fixed version published).
#
# DROPPED in the trixie re-enumeration (present in the prior bookworm
# ledger; not carried inert, because each is gone or no longer a
# CRITICAL/HIGH finding on trixie):
#   * CVE-2023-45853 (zlib/minizip) — RESOLVED in trixie (zlib 1:1.3.dfsg-2;
#     image ships 1:1.3.dfsg+really1.3.1-1+b1).
#   * CVE-2025-7458 (libsqlite3-0) — RESOLVED in trixie (fixed 3.42.0-1;
#     image ships 3.46.1-7+deb13u1).
#   * CVE-2025-59375 / CVE-2026-25210 / CVE-2026-45186 (expat) — `libexpat1`
#     is NOT an installed dpkg package in the trixie image, so trivy reports
#     no OS-level expat finding. (Python's pyexpat path remains routed
#     through defusedxml; that mitigation is unaffected.)
#   * CVE-2026-48959 (perl) — not a CRITICAL/HIGH finding on the trixie image
#     under the current vuln DB; left to the gate so any future escalation
#     re-surfaces for review rather than being pre-accepted here.
#
# Common mitigations (all 8): the container runs as non-root UID 1000 with
# SUID/SGID bits stripped, and none of these components sits on an
# untrusted-input path in the shipped API (per-entry rationale below). The
# entries are genuinely irreducible: libsqlite3-0 and ncurses
# (libtinfo6/libncursesw6/ncurses-base/ncurses-bin) are linked by CPython
# itself (the sqlite3 and readline/curses stdlib modules) and cannot be
# removed without removing the interpreter; perl-base is pulled by apt's own
# adduser dependency. Debian trixie publishes no patched build to
# `apt-get upgrade` to. Each is accepted only as a time-boxed, enumerated,
# expiring risk — never silenced.

# SQLite (libsqlite3-0 3.46.1-7+deb13u1) — Debian trixie status "open", no
# upstream fix yet (fixed only in SQLite >= 3.53.2). CVE-2026-11822 and
# CVE-2026-11824 are FTS5 full-text-search memory-corruption bugs reachable
# only by opening an attacker-crafted database through the FTS5 extension,
# which Mercury never does (no SQLite-backed feature ships by default;
# DATABASE_URL persistence is optional and is not SQLite-FTS5).
CVE-2026-11822 exp:2026-09-16
CVE-2026-11824 exp:2026-09-16

# ncurses (libncursesw6 / libtinfo6 / ncurses-base / ncurses-bin
# 6.5+20250216-2) — Debian trixie status "open", no fix. Buffer overflow in
# terminal handling only; never exposed to untrusted input in the API
# container.
CVE-2025-69720 exp:2026-09-16

# perl-base 5.40.1-6 — pulled in by Debian essential tooling (adduser); the
# API container executes no Perl. Debian trixie status "open", no fix.
# CRITICAL: CVE-2026-8376 (Perl heap buffer overflow) and CVE-2026-42496
# (Archive::Tar path traversal). HIGH: CVE-2026-42497 (Archive::Tar hardlink
# extraction), CVE-2026-48962 (IO-Compress arbitrary code execution), and
# CVE-2026-9538 (Archive::Tar memory exhaustion). All require Mercury to run
# Perl over an attacker-controlled archive/stream, which it never does.
CVE-2026-8376 exp:2026-09-16
CVE-2026-42496 exp:2026-09-16
CVE-2026-42497 exp:2026-09-16
CVE-2026-48962 exp:2026-09-16
CVE-2026-9538 exp:2026-09-16

# Mesa GL stack (CVE-2026-40393): REMOVED, not accepted. It was installed
# only as OpenCV's libGL dependency, but Mercury uses
# opencv-python-headless (no libGL linkage) and makes no cv2 GUI calls, so
# the Dockerfile no longer installs libgl1-mesa-glx — the package and its
# CVE are gone from the image. The blocking Trivy gate (ignore-unfixed:
# false) re-verifies this on every build: were the package to reappear,
# the gate would fail rather than silently pass.
